0, "environment": 0.0, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": null, "sigma_race": 9.3, "sigma_total": 11.51, "baseline_margin": 10.0, "analyst_adjustment": 0.0, "key_factors": ["Washington's D+10 partisan lean is the strongest available signal in an open race", "Term-limited incumbent means no incumbency advantage for either party", "No state polls, expert ratings, or fundraising data yet, keeping uncertainty wide", "National environment and eventual nominees could shift the margin"], "rationale": "With no polls, expert ratings, or market prices available, the rating rests on Washington's Democratic-leaning fundamentals; the term-limited incumbent removes any incumbency effect, so a roughly ten-point Democratic edge with wide uncertainty earns Likely D rather than Safe D.", "watch": "Early state polls, expert ratings, or the emergence of an unusually strong or polarizing nominee on either side could move the margin several points in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Washington sits about ten points to the left of the country as a whole, and that partisan lean is the main force shaping this open presidential race. With the incumbent president term-limited, neither party gets an incumbency advantage, so the contest starts as a fight over a state that has consistently backed Democrats in recent presidential elections. No candidate filings, fundraising totals, or state polls have yet clarified how the national tickets will perform here.\n\nWithout polling or expert ratings, the rating rests entirely on the state's fundamentals, leaving wide uncertainty around a double-digit Democratic edge. A strong Republican nominee or a favorable national environment could narrow the gap, while continued Democr